%U A401857 29,127,3,11,31,3,131,137,3,139,149,111,2,17,506,151,157,41,163,167,43,173,179,181,191
%N A401857 Lexicographically earliest sequence of positive integers such that a(n) is the least positive integer j for which gcd(a(n), a(n+j)) > 1.
%C A401857 Say that a(k) arrives at n if k + a(k) = n. Then a(n) is the product of the least prime factors of the terms arriving at n or, if no term arrives at n, the least prime that divides none of the a(k) with k < n < k + a(k). Hence all terms are squarefree, the primes first appear in increasing order, and choosing each term as small as possible never leads to a dead end (proofs in the Additional comments).
%C A401857 A prime term a(n) = p recurs as a(n+p) = p unless another term also arrives at n+p, so a prime keeps recurring with period p until such a collision.
%C A401857 If a(k) is even, then a(n) is odd for k < n < k + a(k), and a(k+a(k)) is even by the rule above. Hence the even terms are exactly the a(e(j)), where e(1) = 1 and e(j+1) = e(j) + a(e(j)); the e(j) are odd and begin 1, 3, 5, 11, 13, 27, 61, 63, 569, 34854471. The last even term with n <= 10^9 is a(386414683) = 2*54650201*363670211, so a(n) is odd for 386414683 < n < 39749300644139505. Of the 50 even terms with n <= 10^9, 41 equal 2; hence 2 occurs exactly 41 times among the first 39749300644139504 terms, the last time at n = 386414681.
%C A401857 Of the first 10^9 terms, 993738203 are prime, and 877856895 of these are primes that have not occurred earlier. Since a(857614665) = 3*279941131, no a(n) with 857614665 < n < 1697438058 is divisible by 3, so the value 3 occurs exactly 93 times among the first 1697438057 terms, the last time at n = 857614662. None of 10, 15, 21, 22, 26, 30 occurs among the first 10^9 terms. The largest of the first 10^9 terms is the 48-digit a(223768247) = 31634983*44120567*54970973*72904723*108230033*210169979.
%C A401857 Proofs, computation details, tables of notable terms, and open questions are in the Additional comments.
%H A401857 Alper Ferudun, <a href="/A401857/b401857.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..10000</a>
