%H A401857 Alper Ferudun, <a href="/A401857/a401857_1.txt">Rust program</a>.
%e A401857 Since gcd(1, m) = 1 for every m, no term is 1; so a(1) = 2. Then a(1) = 2 requires a(2) to be odd, so a(2) = 3. At n = 3, a(1) = 2 arrives, so a(3) is even, while a(2) = 3 requires a(3) to be coprime to 3; so a(3) = 2. No term arrives at 4, and a(4) must be coprime to a(2) = 3 and a(3) = 2; so a(4) = 5.
%e A401857 At n = 5 both a(2) = 3 and a(3) = 2 arrive, while a(4) = 5 requires a(5) to be coprime to 5; so a(5) = 6. The next terms 7, 11, 13, 5, 17 are coprime to 6, and only a(5) arrives at 11, so a(11) = 2, the least prime factor of 6.
%e A401857 The first six occurrences of 5 are at n = 4, 9, 14, 19, 24, 29. At n = 34 both a(29) = 5 and a(21) = 13 arrive, so a(34) = 5*13 = 65; then 5 returns at n = 34 + 65 = 99 as the least prime factor of 65.
